    "title": "Trees, Heaps & Graphs",
    "summary": "Tree traversals, BST invariants, heaps, graph search including topological sort, and tries.",
    "conceptNotes": [
      {
        "id": "note-trie",
        "title": "Tries (prefix trees)",
        "summary": "A trie is a tree where each edge is labeled with one character, so the path from the root to any node spells out a prefix. Words that share a prefix share that path, which is what makes prefix search and autocomplete fast: you walk one character at a time instead of scanning every string in the set.",
        "whenToUse": [
          "Prefix matching — \"every word starting with ...\"",
          "Autocomplete / typeahead search",
          "Word search over a fixed dictionary (combine with DFS/backtracking)",
          "Anywhere a hash set of strings would work, but you also need prefix queries"
        ],
        "coreOperations": [
          "insert(word) — walk from the root, creating a child node per character that doesn't already exist, then mark the last node as end-of-word",
          "search(word) — walk the same path; return false if any character is missing, otherwise return whether the final node is marked end-of-word",
          "startsWith(prefix) — same walk as search, but return true as soon as the path exists, regardless of the end-of-word mark"
        ],
        "complexity": "Insert and search are O(L) in the length of the word, independent of how many words are stored — that's the whole appeal over a hash set, which is also O(L) per lookup but can't answer prefix queries without scanning every key."
      }
    ],
